From 43f929832bc1629b9c0fd7f3a169bca5f5b58d71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: danilo neves cruz <cruzdanilo@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 18:53:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: evaluate web scripts in an isolated world

Pages that lock down their globals, such as extension pages under LavaMoat, reject Maestro's script ("property \"maestro\" of globalThis is inaccessible under scuttling mode"), so no element on them can be read. Evaluate in an isolated world instead: it shares the page's DOM but not its globals, and the same world name returns the same context for each document. Properties that page scripts define on custom elements don't exist in that world, so a resource id is read from the attribute whenever its property is missing. A navigation can commit between creating the world and evaluating in it. A numeric execution context id is only unique within a renderer, so after a cross-site commit the old id can name a context of another frame in the new renderer, and the evaluation would answer from there. Evaluate by the world's unique context id instead, kept for the frame until it stops resolving, so reading a known document takes one round trip. A replaced document then fails the evaluation with "uniqueContextId not found" or "Cannot find context with specified id" before the script has run, depending on whether the old context still resolves, and the script runs in the next document. A document that goes away during the evaluation, as on a page that reloads while it parses, answers "Inspected target navigated or closed" or "Execution context was destroyed." instead, and the script may already have run, so it runs in the next document once more at most, then fails as a navigation, which the driver retries. When the frame has no unique id yet or it stopped resolving, enable Runtime before creating the world, so the world's own Runtime.executionContextCreated carries its unique id in the same round trip and the evaluation runs right after, before a page that keeps replacing its document drops it again. If that document was replaced too, wait for the execution context of the one that replaced it, then create the world there and evaluate in it. When the target id stops naming a frame, look up the main frame in the frame tree and keep it for the target, since a prerendered page keeps its target when it activates but brings a main frame of its own. All of it, the read by a known unique id included, shares one response timeout, so a page that keeps replacing its document faster than one evaluation fails once it passes, like a target that never answers, and the error says how many documents were replaced on the way. A session that fails while Runtime may still be enabled on it is dropped, so its events never reach a later command.
